## Case 2: The Client-Side Error / Validation Sink (CSTI Framework Injection)
This configuration simulates a frontend framework environment (like Vue or AngularJS) handling real-time validation feedback. If a login input errors out, the framework reflects the parameter value inside an expression box, inadvertently evaluating the bracket expressions.

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Authentication Portal - Validation Error Lab</title>
    <!-- Simulating an older responsive framework engine prone to expressions parsing -->
    <script src="https://jsdelivr.net"></script>
    <style>
        body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background: #eec; padding: 50px; }
        .login-box { background: white; padding: 30px; border-radius: 8px; max-width: 400px; margin: 0 auto; }
        .error-banner { background: #f8d7da; color: #721c24; padding: 10px; border-radius: 4px; margin-bottom: 15px; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div id="app" class="login-box">
        <h2>System Authentication</h2>
        
        <!-- DANGEROUS REFLECTION CONTAINER: Input is echoed inside a framework template node -->
        <div v-if="errorActive" class="error-banner">
            ❌ Verification Failed for parameter context: <span id="error-output"></span>
        </div>

        <form @submit.prevent>
            <input type="password" placeholder="Enter account credentials" style="width:100%; padding:10px; margin-bottom:10px;">
            <button style="width:100%; padding:10px; background:#007bff; color:white; border:none;">Proceed</button>
        </form>
    </div>

    <script>
        const urlParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
        const errorContext = urlParams.get('password'); // Resolves to "${alert(1)}"

        // Instantiate the frontend tracking app
        const app = new Vue({
            el: '#app',
            data: {
                errorActive: false
            }
        });

        if (errorContext) {
            app.errorActive = true;
            // The input string lands inside the DOM right before Vue compiles the layout wrapper
            document.getElementById('error-output').innerHTML = errorContext;
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>

