## Case 3: Form Action Hijacking (Structural Property Overwrite)
In this case, the script handles the query input inside an active DOM object reference. If an attacker passes a script payload, it can dynamically re-write where data is submitted, altering the properties of the web template completely.

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Enterprise Login Gateway</title>
    <style>
        body { font-family: sans-serif; padding: 50px; text-align: center; }
        .form-container { display: inline-block; text-align: left; padding: 20px; border: 1px solid #ddd; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="form-container">
        <h3>Corporate SSO Sign-In</h3>
        
            <input type="text" name="user" placeholder="Employee ID" style="display:block; margin-bottom:10px; padding:5px;">
            <input type="password" name="pass" placeholder="Password" style="display:block; margin-bottom:10px; padding:5px;">
            <button type="submit">Authorize Session</button>
        </form>
    </div>

    <script>
        const queryParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
        const trackingRef = queryParams.get('password');

        if (trackingRef) {
            // HIGHLY VULNERABLE STRUCTURAL SINK: Appending raw inputs directly into a script evaluation loop
            // An attacker can exploit this logic to break out of string borders and rewrite elements globally.
            try {
                const debugSession = {};
                eval(`debugSession.reference = "${trackingRef}";`);
            } catch(e) {
                // Prevent crash notifications from showing to the user
            }
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>

